# Guestbook

A guestbook that works with JavaScript switched off. Every page is rendered on the
server and every change is an ordinary form POST, so this project ships no WebAssembly
at all — the other end of the range from a reactive page in `pages/`.

## A Signing has:
    a public who, which is Text
    a public body, which is Text

## Note
Where the book is kept. One line per signing, `who|body` — the server's PRIVATE
format, never the wire.

## To storePath () -> Text:
    Return "guestbook.txt".

## To signings () -> Seq of Signing:
    Let mutable out be a new Seq of Signing.
    Let mutable raw be "".
    Inspect read(storePath()):
        When Ok (t):
            Set raw to t.
        When Err (e):
            Set raw to "".
    Repeat for line in lines(raw):
        If length of text_bytes(line) is greater than 0:
            Let parts be split(line, "|").
            If length of parts is at least 2:
                Push (a new Signing with who (item 1 of parts) and body (item 2 of parts)) to out.
    Return out.

## To signIf (ok: Bool) and (who: Text) and (body: Text) -> Bool:
    If ok is equal to false:
        Return false.
    Return sign(who, body).

## To sign (who: Text) and (body: Text) -> Bool:
    Let mutable raw be "".
    Inspect read(storePath()):
        When Ok (t):
            Set raw to t.
        When Err (e):
            Set raw to "".
    Set raw to raw + "{who}|{body}\n".
    Inspect write(storePath(), raw):
        When Ok (v):
            Return true.
        When Err (e):
            Return false.

## Note
The whole site is two routes. `GET /` draws the page; the form posts to `/say`, which
appends a line and answers with a page saying so, linking back to `/`. No JavaScript is
involved at any point.

## Note
A visitor's session. Declaring one is what makes the router CHECK the form's token: a
`POST` whose `_csrf` field is not the one `csrf()` minted for this visitor is answered
`403` before `/say` runs. The token is bound to a per-visitor nonce the session carries,
so one visitor's token is worthless to anyone else. The session also remembers the name a
visitor last signed with, so the form offers it back.

## Session
    a name, which is Text.

## Route GET / -> View:
    <main>
      <h1>Guestbook</h1>
      <ul id="signings">
        {for each s in signings():}<li><b>{s's who}</b> &mdash; {s's body}</li>{end}
      </ul>
      <form method="post" action="/say">
        <input type="hidden" name="_csrf" value={csrf()}>
        <label>Your name <input name="who" value={the session's name}></label>
        <label>Your message <input name="body"></label>
        <button type="submit">Sign the book</button>
      </form>
    </main>

## Note
What a signing answers: a page saying whether it went in, with the way back.

## To answer (saved: Bool) and (who: Text) -> View:
    <main>
      {if saved:}
        <h1>Signed</h1>
        <p>Thank you, {who}.</p>
      {else:}
        <h1>Not signed</h1>
        <p>Give a name and a message.</p>
      {end}
      <a href="/">Back to the book</a>
    </main>

## Route POST /say (who: Text) and (body: Text) -> View:
    Let saved be signIf(who is not equal to "" and body is not equal to "", who, body).
    If saved:
        Set the session's name to who.
    Return answer(saved, who).

## Test: the book shows what it holds

Given a file "guestbook.txt" containing "Ada|hello\n".
Expect that route("GET", "/") contains "<li><b>Ada</b>".
Expect that route("GET", "/") contains "hello</li>".

## Test: the form carries this visitor's token

Expect that route("GET", "/") contains "name=\"_csrf\"".

## Main

Serve on port 8080.
